WEBVTT

00:02.350 --> 00:05.943
Your agent sounds sure, but it doesn't know your project.

00:06.602 --> 00:10.795
So you explain it again, every session, every tool.

00:11.384 --> 00:15.047
What you wrote down is scattered, stale, and unverifiable.

00:16.633 --> 00:18.088
Meet Open Knowledge.

00:18.088 --> 00:21.726
One knowledge base for your team and every agent.

00:22.409 --> 00:24.730
Start with the docs you already have.

00:24.730 --> 00:28.745
One command turns plain markdown into a managed knowledge base.

00:28.745 --> 00:29.560
No docs yet?

00:29.560 --> 00:31.066
Your agent drafts them.

00:32.370 --> 00:35.616
Search it, and every answer comes with receipts.

00:35.616 --> 00:38.389
The file, the lines, and a content hash.

00:38.389 --> 00:41.433
Your agent stops guessing and starts citing.

00:42.357 --> 00:44.179
One wiki for every agent.

00:44.179 --> 00:46.364
Any MCP client can search it.

00:46.364 --> 00:49.133
Switch tools and keep your knowledge.

00:51.870 --> 00:55.053
When things change, your agent proposes the edit.

00:55.053 --> 00:57.131
You review the diff, then save.

00:57.131 --> 00:58.820
The history lives in Git.

01:00.472 --> 01:03.286
Checks catch stale knowledge before it ships.

01:03.286 --> 01:05.350
When a source changes, you know.

01:05.350 --> 01:08.539
And every pull request is tested before it merges.

01:11.076 --> 01:13.148
Then ship knowledge like code.

01:13.148 --> 01:17.084
Every release is immutable, and rollback is one command.

01:17.900 --> 01:18.866
One lifecycle.

01:18.866 --> 01:21.487
From a folder of notes to production.

01:21.487 --> 01:22.521
No migrations.

01:26.396 --> 01:27.459
Open Knowledge.

01:27.459 --> 01:30.577
Build a knowledge base your agents can use.

01:30.577 --> 01:32.773
Start today at openknowledge .

01:32.773 --> 01:32.986
sh.
